 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T19:07+08:00",
 "answer": "Whether the haze in Singapore will improve depends on changing wind patterns and weather conditions. \n\nAccording to the National Environment Agency (NEA):\n* **Favorable Factors:** Showers are forecast over Singapore and parts of Kalimantan, and winds are expected to gradually shift and weaken (blowing more from the south or southeast). This shift could help reduce the direct transport of dense smoke haze from Kalimantan. \n* **Remaining Risks:** Dry conditions are still persisting in parts of the southern ASEAN region, and some smoke haze from Sumatra may still drift toward the island. \n\nWhile overall conditions have shown some signs of easing compared to previous peaks, air quality remains fluid. For your immediate plans, it is best to check the real-time **1-hour PM2.5 concentrations** on the **myENV app** or [www.haze.gov.sg](https://www.haze.gov.sg) before heading outdoors.",
